-- title:
--   ordᵥ(df) = ordᵥ(f) - 1 at zeros and poles
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field of characteristic zero, $F$ a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure, and suppose some $x \in F$ is such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Let $v$ be a place of $F$ over $K$ in the sense of the structure `Place`, that is, a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, distinct from all of $F$, and a principal ideal ring; write $\operatorname{ord}_v$ for the associated normalised integer valuation, obtained as minus the logarithm of the $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued adic valuation of the height-one prime of $v$. For $\omega \in \Omega[F\,⁄\,K]$ put $\operatorname{ordDiff}_v(\omega) = \operatorname{ord}_v(g)$, where $t$ is a chosen element with $\operatorname{ord}_v(t) = 1$ and $g$ is a chosen element of $F$ with $\omega = g \cdot D_{K/F}(t)$ (both choices default to $0$ if no such element exists). Then for every $f \in F$ with $\operatorname{ord}_v(f) \neq 0$ one has $\operatorname{ordDiff}_v(D_{K/F}(f)) = \operatorname{ord}_v(f) - 1$, a single formula covering both zeros and poles of $f$.
--
--   This is the local computation of the order of a differential at a zero or pole, in the form $\operatorname{ord}_v(df) = \operatorname{ord}_v(f) - 1$ for $\operatorname{ord}_v(f) \neq 0$, valid in characteristic zero where no $p$-th power phenomenon can occur. It feeds the computation of canonical divisors and hence the genus of modular curves, being used in the identification of the genus of the modular function field with the genus formula and in the study of regular differentials there.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Place_ordDiff_D_eq_ord_sub_one.lean

